implementationPlan.description:
THE INCIDENT SEQUENCE AND THE REGISTRATION ARM (DoD 1, and the composition proof).

`health-incident.test.ts` - ONE CONTINUOUS SEQUENCE against a real store, counting incidents at every stage:
1. two failures -> ZERO incidents (an off-by-one here pages on every transient blip);
2. the third consecutive failure -> EXACTLY ONE;
3. TEN MORE failures -> STILL EXACTLY ONE. DoD 1 names ten specifically: an arm checking only "an incident exists" passes for a loop opening one per probe, invisible until it floods the queue;
4. recovery -> CLOSED, open count zero, the closed record surviving;
5. a SECOND outage -> a NEW incident. Closing must not mean suppressing.
Plus an INTERMITTENT pattern - fail, fail, succeed, fail, fail, fail - opening exactly one incident at the sixth probe and none earlier. A non-resetting counter passes the simple case and then pages forever on blips.
Assert the incident SURVIVES ring eviction: age the ring past its bound and confirm the incident is still readable. An outage record that disappears with its evidence is worse than none.

THE REGISTRATION ARM (the second defect's proof), in the composition's own test: assert the probe job IS REGISTERED on the durable schedule after composition - by id - and that the callback aborts when the signal fires. Registration is the whole point of step 5; without an arm, a future refactor drops the `register()` call and nothing goes red.

MUTATE AND CONFIRM RED: remove the existing-open-incident check so it opens per failure, run the sequence, confirm stage 3 reds; restore. All new files are UNTRACKED, so back up out of tree and prove restores by SHA256 in a call separate from the restore.

